 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "The percent equation",
   "span": "full",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Every percent calculation is one equation: $\\text{part}=\\frac{\\text{percent}}{100}\\times\\text{whole}$ $\\;$ (**“of” = times**). The three versions are the same equation, with a different unknown."
    },
    {
     "type": "rules",
     "head": [
      "Find",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "**the part**",
       "$45\\%$ of $80$ is what? $\\ \\frac{45}{100}\\cdot80=36$"
      ],
      [
       "**the percent**",
       "$21$ is what percent of $75$? $\\ \\frac{21}{75}\\cdot100=28\\%$ $\\;$ (A is what percent of B: $\\ \\frac{A}{B}\\times100$)"
      ],
      [
       "**the whole**",
       "$30\\%$ of what is $63$? $\\ \\frac{30}{100}\\cdot x=63,\\ x=210$"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Percents and fractions",
   "col": "L",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "rules",
     "head": [
      "Fraction",
      "Percent"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "$\\frac12,\\ \\frac14,\\ \\frac18$",
       "$50\\%,\\ 25\\%,\\ 12.5\\%$ $\\;$ (halve each time)"
      ],
      [
       "$\\frac1{10},\\ \\frac15,\\ \\frac1{20}$",
       "$10\\%,\\ 20\\%,\\ 5\\%$"
      ],
      [
       "$\\frac13,\\ \\frac16$",
       "$33\\frac13\\%,\\ 16\\frac23\\%$ $\\;$ (a sixth: “sixteen and a bit”)"
      ],
      [
       "$\\frac38,\\ \\frac58$",
       "$37.5\\%,\\ 62.5\\%$ $\\;$ (three, five times $12.5$)"
      ],
      [
       "$\\frac35,\\ \\frac7{20}$",
       "$60\\%,\\ 35\\%$"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**Percent → fraction:** over $100$, then reduce: $64\\%=\\frac{64}{100}=\\frac{16}{25}$. $\\;$ **Fraction → percent:** expand the bottom to $100$: $\\frac{11}{25}=\\frac{44}{100}=44\\%$."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Three ways to calculate",
   "col": "R",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**The percent equation** (box 1) works every time. Two faster ways:"
    },
    {
     "type": "cols",
     "cols": [
      [
       {
        "type": "fig",
        "ref": "ratio",
        "caption": "**The ratio table:** no whole needed. $15\\%$ is $54$ → $5\\%$ is $18$ → $25\\%$ is $90$."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "**Ten-percent chunks:** $10\\%$ of $240=24$; $\\;30\\%=72$; $\\;5\\%=12$ (half of $10\\%$); $\\;35\\%=84$. $\\;$ Whole missing: $30\\%=63\\Rightarrow10\\%=21\\Rightarrow100\\%=210$."
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "A percent of a percent",
   "col": "L",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**Whole not given?** Plug in $100$. The number you get at the end is the answer in percent. $\\;+80\\%$, then $-25\\%$: $100\\to180\\to135$, up $35\\%$ (not $55\\%$)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**Each new percent is of the new amount.** $\\;-30\\%$, then $-20\\%$: $100\\to70\\to56$, lost $44\\%$."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**Who gets the hundred?** The whole comes right after “of” or “than”, and the one that decides is in the question."
    },
    {
     "type": "fig",
     "ref": "tree",
     "caption": "**The percent tree:** each branch down is a new $100\\%$; multiply along the path: $20\\%$ of $65\\%=13\\%$."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**Up and down by the same percent** never comes back: a loss of $\\frac{p^2}{100}\\%$ ($\\pm20\\%$: $4\\%$ lower). $\\;$ **A fixed amount** in the story? Then $100$ doesn't work: use the real number or a letter."
    }
   ]
  },
